@page "/docs/common/forms"

@* Forms and validation: one form for static and interactive pages, what fields take from the
   model, checks before and after posting, columns, read-only and disabled forms, and errors.
   From docs/components.md ("Forms") and getting-started.md. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="forms">
    <Lead>
        <Code>Form</Code> wraps Blazor's <Code>EditForm</Code>, so one form works everywhere: a real post on a static page,
        even with JavaScript off, and live validation on an interactive one. Fields take their labels and rules from your
        model.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">A form that posts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Give the form a <Code>FormName</Code>, and the model property <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm]</Code> with the same
            <Code>FormName</Code>, so the post fills it on a static page:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" HighlightLines="1,9-10">@Basic</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>OnValidSubmit</Code> runs on the server once the model is valid, in every mode. On an interactive page the
            same form checks each field as it changes and submits without a page load. On a static page the errors come
            from the last post: once a field's value changes its errors go (in a Combobox or MultiSelect, when an option
            is chosen, not as the search is typed), and the next post checks it again. A <Code>FormErrors</Code> summary
            keeps listing them, and with JavaScript off they all stay until the next post.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Labels and rules from the model</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each field reads the property's attributes: its label from <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code> (or the property name in
            words), the required mark from <Code>[Required]</Code>, the input type from <Code>[EmailAddress]</Code>,
            <Code>[Phone]</Code> or <Code>[Url]</Code>, and limits from <Code>[StringLength]</Code> and <Code>[Range]</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Model</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Without <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>, the label splits the property name into words ("Zip code"), but validation
            messages use it as written ("The ZipCode field is required."). Add <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code> when the messages
            should read like the label.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Checked before and after posting</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser checks required fields, lengths, ranges, emails and URLs before posting. With IzzyBlazor's script it
            uses your attribute's <Code>ErrorMessage</Code>, so it says what the server would; without the script, the
            browser uses its own words. The server checks every rule after
            posting and shows its messages under each field. Rules the browser can't check, such as a custom attribute, run
            only on the server.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Required</Code> on a field marks it and asks the browser to check it, but the server only reads
            <Code>[Required]</Code> on the model: put rules that must hold there.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Columns</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Use <Code>Columns</Code>, up to 4, to lay fields out in columns sized by the form's own width, so one form fits a
            page, a dialog and a drawer. Each field takes a column; <Code>ColSpan="GridSpan.Full"</Code> gives it the row.
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="form-columns" />
        <TextBlock>
            Two columns start at 32rem, three at 48rem and four at 64rem; below that it's one. Anything that isn't a field,
            such as text, <Code>FormErrors</Code> or <Code>FormActions</Code>, takes the whole row. <Code>NewRow</Code>
            starts a field on a new row.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Groups, actions and errors</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/fieldset">Fieldset</Anchor> groups related fields under a <Code>Label</Code>, with its own <Code>Columns</Code> or <Code>Dividers</Code> between rows.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>FormActions</Code> is the row of buttons at the end. Last in a form that fills a dialog or drawer, it becomes the footer and stays in view.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>FormErrors</Code> lists every message under "Please fix the following", and takes focus after a failed submit, with JavaScript off too.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>FieldError For="() =&gt; Model.Email"</Code> shows one property's messages, for an input of your own.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Read-only and disabled</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ReadOnly</Code> on the form makes every field read-only: the values show and post, but don't change, as on a
            page that shows a record before editing it. HTML has no read-only select, checkbox, radio or range, so selects show
            their value in a read-only text field, and checkboxes, switches, radios, segments and sliders are disabled while
            hidden fields post their values.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Disabled</Code> on the form disables every field, and disabled fields post nothing. Neither touches buttons,
            so show the ones that fit. A field's own <Code>Disabled</Code> works the same way, and so does its own
            <Code>ReadOnly</Code> on the fields that have one (text fields and selects; checkboxes, switches, radios, segments and sliders don't).
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Labels and marks</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>RequiredMark</Code> on the form picks the marks: an asterisk on required fields (the default),
            "(optional)" on the others, which reads better when most fields are required, or none.
            <Code>Layout="FormLayout.Horizontal"</Code> puts labels beside the fields, at the start, from 640px up; with
            <Code>Columns</Code> they stay on top.
            <Code>HideLabel</Code> keeps a field's label for screen readers only, as in a one-field search form.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>: every parameter, and <Code>Validator</Code> to replace DataAnnotations.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>: types, add-ons, passwords and validation.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>: the words forms use, such as "(optional)".</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</Anchor>: a post that fails or arrives empty, an empty selection, a redirect after saving.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Basic = """
        <Form Model="Signup" FormName="signup" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync">
            <FormErrors />
            <TextField @bind-Value="Signup.Email" />
            <Select @bind-Value="Signup.Plan" Placeholder="Choose a plan" />
            <Button Submit>Create account</Button>
        </Form>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "signup")]
            public SignupModel? Signup { get; set; }

            protected override void OnInitialized() => Signup ??= new();
        }
        """;

    private const string Model = """
        public sealed class SignupModel
        {
            [Required(ErrorMessage = "Enter your email"), EmailAddress]
            public string? Email { get; set; }      // "Email", type="email", required

            [StringLength(10)]
            public string? ZipCode { get; set; }    // "Zip code", maxlength="10"

            [Required(ErrorMessage = "Choose a plan")]
            public Plan? Plan { get; set; }         // a select of the enum's values
        }
        """;
}
